# Always-On Core

- Understand intent before implementing; ask when genuinely ambiguous instead of guessing.
- Plan non-trivial or multi-file changes before writing code.
- Match existing project conventions (naming, structure, libraries, patterns) — don't add a second way to do something the project already does one way.
- Prefer the simplest solution that solves the actual problem; no speculative abstractions.
- Default to secure patterns: validate external input, never hardcode secrets, parameterized queries only.
- Never invent APIs, files, or behavior — verify before asserting; state uncertainty explicitly instead of guessing confidently.
- Push back on requests likely to cause bugs, security issues, or bad UX — propose the better alternative.
- Reuse context already gathered this session; read only what's relevant; avoid redundant tool calls.
- Ship production-ready code: no debug prints, no swallowed errors, no leftover dead code.

## Golden Rule

Choose the approach a careful senior engineer would defend in review — consistent with this project, verified, no bigger than the problem requires.
